<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Step zero &middot; everything needs it</div>
		<h1>Unlock the bootloader</h1>
		<p class="lede">Xiaomi has to give you the key before the phone will boot
		anything but MIUI. It takes seven days of waiting, so start here even if you
		are only reading. Everything on the phone is erased at the end. No Windows
		needed.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>On the phone</h2></div>
		<ol>
			<li>Put a SIM in and turn <b>mobile data</b> on. The next step will not go
			through over wifi.</li>
			<li><b>Settings &rarr; About phone</b> and tap <b>MIUI version</b> seven
			times. That turns on the developer options.</li>
			<li><b>Settings &rarr; Additional settings &rarr; Developer options
			&rarr; Mi Unlock status</b>, then <b>Add account and device</b>. Sign in
			with your Mi account.</li>
			<li>Wait however long it tells you. The usual answer is <b>168 hours
			&mdash; seven days</b>. The clock runs on Xiaomi's side and nothing you
			do shortens it.</li>
		</ol>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>02</span><h2>A week later, on your computer</h2></div>
		<p>The unlock is a request to Xiaomi's server with your account's token,
		which is why <b>Windows is not required</b>. <b>MiUnlockTool</b> makes that
		request from any of the three systems &mdash; it is a pip package,
		<code>miunlock</code>
		(<a href="https://github.com/offici5l/MiUnlockTool">offici5l/MiUnlockTool</a>),
		and it downloads <code>fastboot</code> itself if you do not have it.</p>
		<p>Put the phone in fastboot first: turn it off, hold <b>volume-down +
		power</b> until it says <i>fastboot</i>, then plug the USB cable in.</p>
		<div class="os">
			<input type="radio" name="unlock-os" id="unlock-linux" checked>
			<input type="radio" name="unlock-os" id="unlock-win">
			<input type="radio" name="unlock-os" id="unlock-mac">
			<div class="tabs">
				<label for="unlock-linux">Linux</label>
				<label for="unlock-win">Windows</label>
				<label for="unlock-mac">macOS</label>
			</div>
			<div class="pane"><div class="sh">
				<div class="bar"><i></i><span>IN A TERMINAL</span></div>
				<pre>pipx install miunlock     <i># or: pip install --user miunlock</i>

sudo fastboot devices     <b># must print your phone</b>
miunlock</pre>
			</div>
			<p>If <code>miunlock</code> says it cannot see the phone while
			<code>sudo fastboot devices</code> can, it is the udev rules: run it as
			<code>sudo env PATH=$PATH miunlock</code>, or install
			<code>android-udev-rules</code> for your distro.</p></div>
			<div class="pane"><div class="sh">
				<div class="bar"><i></i><span>IN POWERSHELL</span></div>
				<pre>pip install miunlock

.\fastboot devices        <i># or let miunlock fetch platform-tools</i>
miunlock</pre>
			</div>
			<p>Windows needs the phone's fastboot driver: the <b>Google USB
			Driver</b>, or Xiaomi's own installer. Xiaomi's
			<a href="https://en.miui.com/unlock/">Mi Unlock</a> is the official
			route here and it works the same way &mdash; log in, phone in fastboot,
			press Unlock.</p></div>
			<div class="pane"><div class="sh">
				<div class="bar"><i></i><span>IN A TERMINAL</span></div>
				<pre>pipx install miunlock     <i># or: pip install --user miunlock</i>

fastboot devices          <b># no sudo needed on macOS</b>
miunlock</pre>
			</div>
			<p>Xiaomi ships no macOS tool, so this is the only route. MiUnlockTool
			downloads the macOS platform-tools by itself the first time.</p></div>
		</div>
		<p>Then, whichever system you used, it walks you through the same four
		things:</p>
		<ol>
			<li>Sign in with the <b>same Mi account</b> you bound the phone to.</li>
			<li>It shows your account's region. Enter to accept.</li>
			<li>It warns that unlocking <b>erases the phone</b>. That is Xiaomi's
			behaviour, not the tool's.</li>
			<li>It gets the token and runs <code>fastboot stage</code> +
			<code>fastboot oem unlock</code>. The phone wipes and reboots into
			MIUI.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>If it refuses, the message comes from Xiaomi's server and says why
		&mdash; usually the week is not up, or the account is not the one bound to
		the phone.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>03</span><h2>Check it worked</h2></div>
		<p>Turn the phone off, hold <b>volume-down + power</b> to get back into
		fastboot, and from your PC:</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>IN A TERMINAL</span></div>
			<pre>sudo fastboot devices              <b># must print your phone</b>
sudo fastboot getvar unlocked      <i># unlocked: yes</i>

<i># Windows: .\fastboot, no sudo. macOS: fastboot, no sudo.</i></pre>
		</div>
		<p>With that <code>yes</code>, every route on this site is open to you.</p>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="install-image.html"><b>Then</b><span>Flash the ready image</span></a>
			<a href="install-pmos.html"><b>Or</b><span>Build a system yourself</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
